  <qti-item-body>
    <qti-feedback-block identifier="show_interaction" outcome-identifier="INTERACTION_VISIBILITY" show-hide="show" class="qti-margin-0">
      <qti-content-body>
        <p>Which sentence uses &quot;cordial&quot; correctly?</p>
    <qti-choice-interaction response-identifier="RESPONSE" shuffle="true" max-choices="1">
      <qti-simple-choice identifier="A">Their cordial standoff at the property line has now lasted eleven years, with glares traded daily over the hedge and not one single word exchanged.</qti-simple-choice><qti-simple-choice identifier="B">The fans&apos; cordial reception shook the whole stadium for ten full minutes, with banners, air horns, and three competing chants going at once.</qti-simple-choice><qti-simple-choice identifier="C">By their tenth anniversary the couple&apos;s letters had grown so deeply cordial that the archive agreed to seal them from public view for fifty years.</qti-simple-choice><qti-simple-choice identifier="D">The rejection letter was unexpectedly cordial: the editor thanked Amaya warmly, praised two passages by name, and asked to see her next story.</qti-simple-choice>
    </qti-choice-interaction>
      </qti-content-body>
    </qti-feedback-block>

    <qti-feedback-block identifier="FB-A" outcome-identifier="FEEDBACK" show-hide="show">
      <qti-content-body><p>Not quite! Cordial means warm and friendly in manner, so it&apos;s not the correct choice here because daily glares and years of silence over a hedge describe a cold war, not warmth of any kind.</p></qti-content-body>
    </qti-feedback-block>
    <qti-feedback-block identifier="FB-B" outcome-identifier="FEEDBACK" show-hide="show">
      <qti-content-body><p>Not quite! Cordial means warmth kept within courteous measure, so it&apos;s not the correct choice here because ten minutes of air horns and competing chants is a joyful eruption far past courtesy — thrilling, but not cordial.</p></qti-content-body>
    </qti-feedback-block>
    <qti-feedback-block identifier="FB-C" outcome-identifier="FEEDBACK" show-hide="show">
      <qti-content-body><p>Not quite! Cordial names friendly warmth that keeps a certain courteous distance, so it&apos;s not the correct choice here because love letters private enough to seal away for fifty years belong to intimacy — a much closer register than cordial describes.</p></qti-content-body>
    </qti-feedback-block>
    <qti-feedback-block identifier="FB-D" outcome-identifier="FEEDBACK" show-hide="show">
      <qti-content-body><p>Correct! Cordial means warm, friendly, and heartfelt in a courteous way, so it&apos;s the correct answer because the editor&apos;s rejection carries real warmth — thanks, named praise, an open door — delivered with perfect professional courtesy.</p></qti-content-body>
    </qti-feedback-block>
  </qti-item-body>
